:"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"partial\",\"rationale\":\"The answer gets the central distinction right. APs create and redeem creation units directly with the fund in the primary market. Market makers post continuous two-sided quotes and provide secondary-market liquidity. It also says market makers need not have an AP agreement, that many firms play both roles, and that market makers use creations and redemptions to offset inventory. It omits the third key point entirely: the lead market maker (LMM), a market maker designated for a particular ETF with specific quoting commitments. It also only implies, rather than states, that market makers who are not APs manage inventory by creating and redeeming through APs. No material factual errors.\"}","ttft_ms":2291,"type":"result","duration_ms":4249,"uuid":"de1fbc47-954f-4946-8724-88fa5486b006","ttft_stream_ms":655,"time_to_request_ms":56,"first_content_frame_ms":655,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
